# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): break plans, thesis and internship hosts.
# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.

# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------

@event break_castle_one_fire
title: The One Lit Fire
pool: castle_routine_repeat4
tone: warm
art: flame
scene: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == "winter": one roaring hearth|one open window}.
text: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == "winter": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.|The steward's dog is in it, and the steward is on the dog's side.}
> left: Talk my way into the armchair
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      mood +small
      guile +tiny
      say: {You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.|You offer the dog your toast. It's a fair trade, and the chair is still warm.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {Nobody moves. Someone says, "Nice try," without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.|The dog growls, the steward sides with the dog, and you take the floor.}
> right: Take the draughty corner
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {season == "winter": {The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.|You find a blanket nobody claims and read until your nose goes numb.}|{It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.|The breeze turns your pages for you, mostly the right way. You read three chapters anyway.}}

@event break_castle_kitchen_rota
title: The Holiday Kitchen
pool: castle_routine
tone: light
art: meal
scene: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.
text: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == "winter": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}
> left: Roll up your sleeves
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {season == "winter": {You stir puddings till your arm aches. "Just like potion work," says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.|The goose fits at last. The cook says it was her idea.}|{A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells "gooseberry" on all of them.|You stone plums all morning. Your fingers are purple until Tuesday.}}
> right: Discover urgent business elsewhere
  guile +tiny
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the village bakery until lunch is over and buy what you could have eaten for free. The cook gives you a meaningful look all evening.|You claim a library emergency. The cook, who knows the library is shut, says nothing and saves you nothing.}

@event break_castle_empty_library
title: The Library on Holiday
pool: castle_routine_repeat4
tone: warm
art: book
tags: study
scene: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.
text: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == "winter": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}
> left: Read whatever I like
  mood +small
  lore +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.|You finish the book you started last break. The bookmark was still where you left it.}
> right: Get ahead on next term
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.|You copy out next term's reading list and get through half of it. The other half looks smug.}}

@event break_castle_post_day
title: Post for the Stay-Behinds
pool: castle_routine
tone: warm
art: letter
scene: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.
text: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == "winter": {Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.|One envelope has your name on it, in handwriting you know.}|{Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says "Wish you were here", and nobody knows whose it is.|One envelope has your name on it and smells of the sea.}}
> left: Write a long letter home
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {final_year: You write that this is your last break at the castle, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.|You write about the porter, the biscuit and the empty hall. It reads like a good life, which it is.}}
> right: Help the porter sort it all
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.|You sort the pile in half the time it took you once. The porter calls you his deputy, and nobody argues.}

# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------

@event break_home_kitchen_table
title: Homework at the Kitchen Table
pool: home_routine_repeat4
slot: morning
tone: warm
art: house
tags: study
scene: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.
text: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == "winter": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {A cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.|Someone has put a book of yours under a wobbly table leg, and it works.}
> left: Study through the noise
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.|You learn to read with one hand and pass the salt with the other. A whole chapter goes in.}
> right: Give up and help with lunch
  mood +small
  say: {You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.|You're on potatoes again. You're told you peel like a scholar, which isn't a compliment.}

@event break_home_the_box
title: The Box Under the Bed
pool: home_routine
tone: warm
art: key
scene: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.
text: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}
> left: Pack it in your trunk
  mood +small
  say: {It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.|You pack it carefully. There's not much left in the box now, and that feels like something ending.}
> right: Give it to your cousin
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {Your cousin, who is applying to the academy next autumn, takes it with enormous seriousness. You feel very old.|Your cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour for a jar of honey.|Your cousin says thank you and puts it in a box of their own, under their own bed. You feel very old and quite happy.}

@event break_home_family_debate
title: The Family Debate
pool: home_routine
slot: morning
tone: light
art: meal
scene: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.
text: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, "Ask the scholar."|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|"The scholar is up," someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == "winter": {Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?|Can a wizard make the hens lay in winter?}|{Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?|Is the old well really haunted, and should it be?}}
> left: Settle it with what I've learned
  chance likely:
    lore +small
    mood +small
    say: {You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.|You settle it with a diagram on the back of the almanac. It's still pinned by the stove the next time you're home.}
  else:
    lore +tiny
    mood -small
    say: {You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.|You're right, and you prove it, and nobody believes you, because the almanac says otherwise.}
> right: Refuse to take sides
  charm +small
  say: {You butter your toast with great care and say, "Hm." It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.|You say both sides have a point. Both sides turn on you. It's the most united the family has been all year.}

@event break_home_village_children
title: The Wizard Is Home
pool: home_routine_repeat4
slot: morning
tone: light
art: sparkle
scene: A gaggle of villagers at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.
text: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. Half the village has found a reason to pass the gate.|The neighbours are back at the gate, with a cousin from the next village to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == "winter": They want more snow for the sledging.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {The baker has brought a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|The miller's wife asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The blacksmith has a stick that might be a wand.}
> left: Show them some sparks
  mana -small
  mood +small
  arcana +tiny
  say: {You make a little burst of light over the gate. The neighbours cheer and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The baker tries to catch it in her apron, or thinks she does, which is the same thing.|You work through the committee's list of sparks, in order. They tick each one off and ask for the list again tomorrow.}
> right: Teach them something real
  charm +small
  say: {You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. The old miller really hears it. You can tell.|You make the blacksmith's stick glow for a minute. It isn't a wand, but he'll never believe that now.}

# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------

@event break_work_holiday_rush
title: The Holiday Rush
plan: work
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: coin
tags: job money
scene: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.
text: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {has(gig_newt): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{has(gig_pell): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{has(gig_post): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{has(gig_library): The library is full of last-minute readers.|{has(gig_kitchens): The Refectory is feeding everyone who stayed, and all their visiting families.|{has(gig_grounds): The grounds are full of visitors walking off their holiday dinners, and every one of them has a question.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}}}} {season == "winter": {Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.|Everyone is in a hurry to be somewhere warm.}|{Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.|The heat slows everyone down except the queue.}}
> left: Smile through it
  charm +small
  gold +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.|You have a smile for every complaint now, sorted by type. The tips follow.}
> right: Take the heavy jobs instead
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.|You know where every heavy thing goes by now. You're done by noon, and nobody has spoken to you. Perfect.}

@event break_work_opening_up
title: Opening Up
pool: work_routine_weight10
when: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)
tone: light
art: key
scene: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.
text: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == "winter": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}
> left: Do it all by the book
  gold +small
  mood -tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.|You open up to the minute, as always. The owner has stopped checking.}
> right: Steal a quiet half hour
  chance likely:
    mood +small
    say: {You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.|There's a chair by the window that's yours now. Half an hour of nothing, and nobody the wiser.}
  else:
    mood -tiny
    gold -small
    say: {The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.|The owner has taken to arriving early just to check, and today it pays off. The half hour comes out of your wages.}

@event break_work_regular
title: The Holiday Regular
pool: work_routine_weight10
when: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)
tone: warm
art: cup
scene: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.
text: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.
> left: Let them finish the story
  charm +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You hear the whole story, standing, while the queue thins out to somewhere else. Your feet ache by the end. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. You stood through all of it, and your back says so.|You know how their stories end by now, and you let them tell it anyway. The queue waits. Your feet don't forgive you.}
> right: Hurry them gently along
  gold +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, "Another time, then," and you both know they mean it.|You keep the queue moving and promise the regular the rest tomorrow. They hold you to it, every time.}

@event break_work_new_hire
title: The Holiday Hire
pool: work_routine_weight10
when: not has(gig_grounds) and not has(gig_post)
tone: light
art: hand
scene: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.
text: {The boss has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}
> left: Show them the ropes
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: {By noon they know where everything is and that the boss is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.|You show them everything you wish someone had shown you. By the end of the day they're showing the next one.}
> right: Let them learn the hard way
  mood +tiny
  gold -tiny
  say: {They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the boss puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway. The boss takes the stock they spoiled out of your pay.|They learn fast, mostly from your mistakes, which they found in the ledger. The boss bills you for the latest one.}

# ---------------- Break: internship (plan: internship) ----------------

@pool internship_routine_repeat5
plan: internship
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event break_internship_lodgings
title: Breakfast at the Lodgings
pool: internship_routine_repeat5
slot: morning
tone: warm
art: bed
tags: home
scene: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.
text: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == "winter": {Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.|Ice on the inside of the window.}|{It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.|The window is open, and someone's breakfast is frying.}} {Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}
> left: Eat a proper breakfast
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.|You eat everything and ask for the recipe. You're late, and your supervisor has started to expect it.}
> right: Skip it and go in early
  lore +small
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.|You go in early with an apple in your pocket. It's gone by the first meeting, and the hunger stays until supper.}

@event break_internship_briefing
title: The Morning Briefing
pool: internship_routine_repeat5
slot: morning
tone: tense
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.
text: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}
> left: Ask what it means
  charm +tiny
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|"Good question," says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.|You ask before anyone else can pretend. Your supervisor answers, and three pens around you start writing.}
> right: Guess and get on with it
  chance even:
    lore +small
    mood +tiny
    say: {You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was "take the rest of the morning off". Lucky. You spend it reading in the archive.|You guess, and you've guessed this one before. The task is done before anyone else has finished asking.}
  else:
    mood -small
    say: {You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.|Your guess is confident, thorough and wrong. Your supervisor reads the result twice and asks you, kindly, to ask next time.}

@event break_internship_other_interns
title: The Other Interns
pool: internship_routine_repeat5
slot: evening
tone: light
art: group
tags: study
scene: A small group of interns eating supper together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.
text: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a supper table.|You've learned to find the supper table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == "winter": Supper is eaten in coats.|Supper is eaten in the last of the sun.}
> left: Join them for supper
  mood +small
  charm +tiny
  gold -tiny
  say: {You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your supper is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.|You sit beside the one who knows where the biscuits are. By pudding you know where everything else is too.}
> right: Eat alone with your notes
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You finish the reading for tomorrow. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.|You eat with your notes again. Someone leaves a biscuit by your elbow without a word.}

@event break_internship_stipend
title: Stipend Day
pool: internship_routine
tone: warm
art: pouch
tags: money
scene: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.
text: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.|The pedlar is back, and has heard about you.}|{season == "winter": {There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.|The baker on the corner has started putting one aside on stipend day.}|{A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.|The baker on the corner sells the last of the cherry buns at noon.}}}
> left: Treat yourself
  mood +small
  gold -small
  say: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.|You buy the pedlar's whole stock of liquorice. He says you're his best customer, and you probably are.}|{season == "winter": {You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.|You spend it on something warm and something sweet, and keep a single coin back for luck.}|{You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.|You buy the last cherry bun and a book you don't need, and eat one while reading the other.}}}
> right: Save it and borrow the book
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You put the envelope away and borrow the book instead. It's the dullest thing you've ever read, and the most useful. You copy out half of it the first night.|The envelope goes under your mattress. You read the borrowed book, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.|The envelope joins the others under the mattress. The pile is getting interesting, and so is the book.}

# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------

@event thesis_margin_notes
title: Notes in the Margin
track: thesis
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.
text: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written "No."|Another chapter back. The margins say "Why?", "Source?" and, once, "Hm."|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says "See me."|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}
> left: Rewrite to his notes
  progress thesis +small
  mood -small
  say: {You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.|You rewrite to his notes without arguing now. It saves a week, and he's usually right, which still annoys you.}
> right: Defend my version
  challenge negotiate using lore vs thorne.lore -2:
    win:
      progress thesis +small
      rel thorne +tiny
      say: {You make your case at his office door. He listens, says "Hm," and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. "Then make it say so," he says, which is almost praise.|You defend the paragraph with a source he hasn't read. He reads it overnight and gives you the paragraph.}
    lose:
      progress thesis -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says "No." again, in smaller letters.|You argue. He agrees with every point and still writes "No." You've begun to find this comforting.}

@event thesis_carrel_neighbour
title: The Next Carrel
track: thesis
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: study
scene: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.
text: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == "winter": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}
> left: Swap drafts for an hour
  chance likely:
    progress thesis +small
    charm +tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, "Oh, I see." You're not sure you did, until now.|You swap drafts over the partition, sandwich crumbs and all. Both chapters come back better.}
  else:
    mood -small
    say: {They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.|Their notes on your chapter are longer than your chapter. You lose the afternoon reading them.}
> right: Hunt a source in the stacks
  lore +small
  progress thesis +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.|You find the source and a better one beside it. You bring both back and share the second.}

@event thesis_is_it_good
title: Is It Any Good?
track: thesis
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: hourglass
tags: study
scene: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.
text: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == "spring": After this term there are no more terms.|{First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.|Someone two carrels down sighs the same sigh.}}
> left: Push on regardless
  progress thesis +small
  mood -small
  say: {You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.|You write through it, as you have before. By evening the doubt has gone quiet, and the page is good.}
> right: Walk by the lake instead
  mood +small
  health +tiny
  progress thesis -tiny
  say: {You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.|You walk the long way round the lake. The doubt can't keep up, and you come back with a sentence that works.}

# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory (place: thesis_lab) ----------------

@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel
title: The Spare Carrel
place: thesis_lab
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: study
scene: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.
text: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == "winter": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering "however" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}
> left: Work until the candle dies
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.|You work until the stub gives out and finish the paragraph by feel. It's better than the ones you could see.}
> right: Just sit in the quiet
  chance likely:
    mood +small
    say: {You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.|You sit in the quiet and the argument rearranges itself while you're not looking. You write it down before it changes its mind.}
  else:
    mood -tiny
    say: {An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. "Thinking," you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.|An advisor finds you sitting still and lectures you on idleness for exactly as long as you'd have sat.}

@event thesis_lab_footnote_war
title: The Footnote War
pool: thesis_lab_routine
tone: light
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.
text: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}
> left: Weigh in with a source
  challenge research expert-:
    win:
      lore +small
      renown +tiny
      say: {You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.|Your source wins the round. The referee chalks a point on the door under a new column, which has your name on it.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      renown -tiny
      say: {Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.|Your source is ruled out of order by the referee, on a technicality you'll be studying for weeks.}
> right: Keep my head down and listen
  lore +small
  say: {You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.|You keep your head down and your notebook open. The war is the best seminar in the building, and it's free.}

@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle
time: evening
title: Borrowing a Candle
pool: thesis_lab_routine
tone: tense
art: candle
tags: study
scene: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.
text: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says "Help yourself" in one hand and "Don't" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}
> left: Borrow one and keep working
  alignment -tiny
  chance likely:
    lore +small
    mood -tiny
    say: {You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says "IOU one candle", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.|You borrow one and replace it the next morning with two. The box owner has started leaving the lid open.}
  else:
    mood -small
    renown -tiny
    say: {The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.|The box is counted now, and the count is short. A note appears asking the borrower to come forward. You don't.}
> right: Go back in the dark
  mood +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.|You know the way back in the dark by now. The argument walks with you and arrives a step ahead.}

# ---------------- Internship: the Royal Library Archives ----------------

