scene: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.
text: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for "the blue one".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}
> left: Take the deep requests
  wage
  perform intern_royal_library +small
  lore +small
  health -small
  say: {Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.|You take the bottom slip without looking at it. Down there, you know the shelves better than the catalogue does.}
> right: Take the near shelves
  wage
  perform intern_royal_library -tiny
  lore +tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and everyone knows you've never been below the third landing.|You clear the near shelves twice over. The long rattles go to someone else, who looks at you as they pass.}

@event intern_royal_library_mending
title: The Mending Table
track: intern_royal_library
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: book
tags: study
scene: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.
text: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder trusts you with the older pages now, which are the worst.} {The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}
> left: Mend it myself
  wage
  challenge research journeyman+ steady:
    win:
      perform intern_royal_library +small
      lore +small
      say: {Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, "Acceptable," which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.|You put the list back together, crossed-out name and all. The binder reads the name and says nothing for a while.}
    lose:
      perform intern_royal_library -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.|The oldest flake crumbles at your touch. The binder writes "lost in repair" in the ledger, in your hand.}
> right: Watch the binder work
  wage
  lore +small
  perform intern_royal_library -tiny
  say: {You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.|You watch the binder mend the page you'd have ruined. Next time, you'll know how. That's the idea.}

# ---------------- Internship: the Conclave Laboratory ----------------

